{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing and expanding his territory. He inherited the county from his father Raymond II and faced constant threats from Muslim forces particularly the Zengi dynasty of Mosul and Aleppo.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1120. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received military training and was involved in political affairs from a young age. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli a strategically important territory on the coast of Syria. He quickly faced challenges consolidating his rule and defending against external enemies. Early in his reign he engaged in conflicts with the Zengids who sought to control Tripoli. He skillfully used diplomacy and military force to maintain his independence.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military activity. He fought numerous battles against the Zengids and other Muslim rulers. He also engaged in campaigns against the Seljuk Turks. He was a skilled military leader and employed a combination of defensive fortifications and offensive strategies to protect his county. He worked to strengthen Tripoli's defenses building castles and fortifying its cities. He also fostered trade and economic prosperity within the county.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a shrewd diplomat. He forged alliances with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch. He also sought to maintain good relations with the Byzantine Empire and the Papacy. He understood the importance of maintaining a network of allies to counter the power of the Zengids. He used diplomacy to secure military assistance and financial support from his allies. He also engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to establish peaceful relations and trade agreements. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in maintaining Tripoli's position in the region.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\n\nIn his later years Raymond III continued to face challenges from the Zengids. He engaged in a series of conflicts with Nur ad-Din Zengi and his successors. He also dealt with internal rebellions and political intrigues. He died in 1199 at the age of approximately 79. He was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli. He was a skilled military leader a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated defender of the Crusader states. His reign helped to secure Tripoli's position as a major power in the Levant for many years. He left behind a legacy of strength and resilience.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.9788545614574105e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0021620250772684813}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.040470559149980545}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.00026222606538794935}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.5258672647178173e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -4.768258077092469e-05}, {"tok